---
code: cmdsub-inner-nonleaf
when-fires: an effect-bearing command runs inside a `$(…)` substitution body, so it has no independent leaf. effect.rs classify (né dq-cmdsub-inner-nonleaf).
why: q-1.f silent-1/silent-4 disclosure; the inner command runs whenever its enclosing line runs. `{inner}` = the resolved inner argv.
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# `id -u` has no leaf of its own: it is part of evaluating the assignment.
uid=$(id -u)
hork --owner "$uid"

-- replay --
$ dorc plan --book=book.sh > /dev/null
classify: note[cmdsub-operand-top]: operand 2 is a runtime-dynamic variable
value (unset or branch-conflicted), so Dorc cannot know its value until the
command runs on the host -- there is nothing to resolve and no read-only probe
to check. Dorc elides a command only when it can prove the command's effect is
already in place, so this one is left to run on every apply.
      [ book.sh:4:14
   4 | hork --owner "$uid"
     |              \____/

   = repair:  Give the operand a value Dorc can resolve statically -- a literal,
              or a variable assigned from one -- so Dorc can probe it and elide
              the command once it has converged. If the value must stay dynamic,
              load an oracle that vouches for this command's convergence, and
              Dorc will guard the command instead of running it every time.
classify: note[cmdsub-inner-nonleaf]: Dorc cannot elide `id -u` on its own: it
runs inside a `$(...)` substitution as part of evaluating its enclosing line, so
it runs whenever that line does.
      [ book.sh:1:1
   1 | #!/bin/sh
     | \___/
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 1 site run unprobed (no read-only check could
be shipped): `hork --owner "$uid"` -- run `dorc why` for the per-site detail
(the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:4:1
   4 | hork --owner "$uid"
     | \_________________/

   = help:  sm site runs `hork --owner "$uid"`
why: ran because operand 2 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value -- its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 4:1 `hork --owner "$uid"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it, make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]
hint: 'hork' (line 4) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
dorc: plan-summary sites=2 elide=0 omit=0 guard=0 run=2 may-alias=0
dorc: decision-digest 9786b3398364eb0426d75b1675f559aa7f0ed4ab3cf04080339d3be1577f80d4

$ dorc-loom --this vars
case: cmdsub-inner-nonleaf
{{position}} = "operand 2"
{{cause}} = "a runtime-dynamic variable\nvalue (unset or branch-conflicted)"
{{inner}} = "id -u"
{{count}} = "1"
{{site_word}} = "site"
{{v0}} = "book.sh"
{{v1}} = "0"
{{v2}} = "0"
{{v3}} = "0"
{{v4}} = "2"
{{v5}} = "0"
